The test file parameterizes 24 calls to `Matrix.LUsolve(b)`, so the relevant path is the LU decomposition used for solving linear systems. In `_LUdecomposition_Simple`, every tested matrix is nonempty, so the zero-shape early return is skipped. The function always executes initialization, enters the main `for pivot_row ...` loop, performs pivot search with `_find_reasonable_pivot_naive`, computes `candidate_pivot_row`, updates any simplified pivot entries, runs elimination over rows and columns, increments `pivot_col`, and finally reaches the trailing `rankcheck` check and normal return. Some test matrices start with a zero leading entry, so the row-swap block is exercised as well. None of the tests use rank-deficient matrices, so the rank-deficiency `ValueError` branches and the early “all remaining submatrix entries are zero” return are not covered.

